SPOILER ALERT   {{{... eating guide for the dungeon ...}}}

it works something like this:

1) unless the corpse is intrinsically old (i.e. gnome or zombie corpse)
    you will get 50 moves (minimum) before the corpse may become tainted.
    if you're sped then you'll get proportionally more minimum time.

    in 50 moves, you can burn off calories in various ways, so it is
    rare that you'll need to eat a "valued" corpse while satiated, but
    even if you do decide to do this, the results don't have to be fatal.

2) you can eat anything safely, no matter how big, if you're not
    satiated.  you will not choke, unless you don't heed the warnings
    in this case!

3) if you eat a filling meal and you _weren't_ satiated when you
    started, then the following things can happen:
    a) you will first get an indication that you're full (Satiated).
        in light of the above, you should read the absence of this
        flag to mean that it is safe to eat.  given the flag, it
        means eating can be a problem.
    b) if the meal was really filling then when you pass thru a
        pre-defined limit you will get a one-time warning ("You're
        having a hard time getting this down") that means if you
        eat shortly thereafter you will certainly choke (after all,
        you were having a hard time getting it all down and you are
        already Satiated.

    c) if the meal was especially filling and you reach a second
        limit you're given a chance to stop eating.  if you elect
        to continue then you may choke, maybe not ...

        as always, in time you become hungrier but there is no
        reciprocal indication when you pass below the choke limits.
        you're only clue is the satiated flag and you have to wait
        until it disappears before safely eating again (or develop
        a good sense of how fast food is burned off and how filling
        a meal might be).

4) if you eat a filling meal and you _were_ satiated when you
    started, then the following things can happen:
    a) when you pass thru the pre-defined lower limit mentioned above
        then you'll get the same warning.  this assumes you were
        under the limit to begin with.
    b) if the meal was so filling that you pass thru the higher limit
        then you will choke with no oppurtunity to stop.  again this
        assumes you were below the lower limit to begin with.
    c) if you were above the lower limit when you started to eat again
        (i.e. you ignored the satiated flag and you ignored your one-
        time while satiated warning) then you choke.

there are other issues but i think i got the above right (it's been
awhile since i've choked or played with the choking stuff).  i don't
find the eating code to be unfair since it tells you, in effect, when
it is safe to eat and when it isn't, but i do find it somewhat amusing
that people make arguments like "how often do people do this?".  i
don't know many people who eat dragon corpses ...

